{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And his offering</b> — extra vav on account of six sons who issued from him, and all were blessed with six blessings. Six sons issued from Nahshon, each possessing six blessings, and they are: David, Messiah, Daniel, Hananiah, Mishael, and Azariah. David, concerning whom it is written: \"skilful in playing, and a mighty valiant man, and a man of war, and prudent in matters, and a comely person, and YHWH is with him\" — skilful in playing: he knows how to ask. And a mighty valiant man: he knows how to answer. A man of war: he knows how to give and take in the war of Torah. And prudent in matters: he understands one matter from within another. And a comely person: he shows a face in halakhah. And YHWH is with him: the halakhah follows him in every place. Messiah, concerning whom it is written: \"and the spirit of YHWH shall rest upon him, the spirit of wisdom and understanding, the spirit of counsel and might, the spirit of knowledge and the fear of YHWH.\" Daniel, Hananiah, Mishael, and Azariah, concerning whom it is written: \"children in whom is no blemish, but well favoured, and skilful in all wisdom, and cunning in knowledge, and understanding science, and such as had ability in them to stand in the king's palace.\" Another matter: why \"and his offering\" with an extra vav? Corresponding to six things that were renewed on that day in the world: first for the Shekhinah — that it rested in Israel; first for the princes; first for the priestly blessing; first for the partition of the camp of the Shekhinah; first for the prohibition of bamot; first for the descent of fire. The son of Nahshon comes with six measures and lays the foundation of the Temple. And why is his name called Nahshon? Because he descended first into the wave of the sea. In all of them it is written \"nasi\" except for Judah, for that tribe is accustomed to humble itself: \"Let your servant, I pray you, remain instead of the lad,\" and likewise concerning David it says: \"he is the smallest.\" In each one there are six verses, corresponding to the six days of creation, and in each one his name is doubled — behold twenty-four, corresponding to the twenty-four hours of day and night. \"A hundred and thirty shekels\" — corresponding to the years of a man until Seth was born." "<b>Seventy shekels</b> — corresponding to seventy [years] that he left to his sons [perhaps meaning to King David, peace be upon him], and to deliver them from seventy nations." "<b>Seventy</b> shekels. In gematria: שבעים נפש (seventy souls), on account of the seventy who went down to Egypt."], :book :numbers, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The phrase 'יבא בן נחשון' is rendered hyper-literally as 'the son of Nahshon comes'; the referent and syntactic relationship to the preceding list is unclear in the source."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"]], :timestamp "2026-07-12T19:21:46.651274508Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}